Q: How to conditionally bypass AdMob Mediation and initialize Unity Ads directly on Huawei devices (No GMS)? A: <p>To solve this, you must stop relying on AdMob Mediation for your Huawei users and instead implement a <strong>GMS Gateway Check</strong> at launch.</p>
<p>Since the Unity Ads SDK does <em>not</em> strictly require Google Play Services to function, it will run perfectly on an HMS device if you call it directly.</p>
<p>Here is the Kotlin implementation using <code>GoogleApiAvailability</code> to detect the device environment and route the initialization accordingly.</p>
<p><strong>1. The Gateway Logic (in your Activity):</strong></p>
<p>Kotlin</p>
<pre><code>import com.google.android.gms.common.ConnectionResult
import com.google.android.gms.common.GoogleApiAvailability
import com.unity3d.ads.UnityAds
import com.google.android.gms.ads.MobileAds

// Set a flag to track which network you are using
private var isUsingUnityAds = false 

val gmsStatus = GoogleApiAvailability.getInstance().isGooglePlayServicesAvailable(this)

if (gmsStatus == ConnectionResult.SUCCESS) {
    isUsingUnityAds = false
    // GMS is present (Standard Android): Initialize AdMob Mediation
    MobileAds.initialize(this) {
        // Load AdMob banners and interstitials here
    }
} else {
    isUsingUnityAds = true
    // GMS is missing (Huawei): Bypass AdMob entirely and initialize Unity Ads
    val unityGameID = &quot;YOUR_UNITY_GAME_ID&quot;
    UnityAds.initialize(applicationContext, unityGameID, false, this)
}
</code></pre>

Q: SAS Enterprise Guide 8.3 - Send Automated Email If Process Breaks A: <p>This is absolutely possible and a very common pattern in SAS automation.</p>
<p>The issue you are running into is that <strong><code>&amp;SYSCC</code> resets between individual steps</strong>, so a successful Step 12 will overwrite an error that happened in Step 2.</p>
<p>The cleanest, bulletproof way to fix this is to use a <strong>Global Flag</strong> that tracks errors across all 12 steps. Here is the exact code you need:</p>
<p>1. Initialize the flag at the very start of your process:</p>
<p>sas</p>
<pre><code>%global process_failed;
%let process_failed = 0;
</code></pre>
<p>Use code with caution.</p>
<p>2. Insert this quick check after your timeout-prone data pulls:</p>
<p>sas</p>
<pre><code>%if &amp;syscc. &gt; 4 %then %let process_failed = 1;
</code></pre>
<p>Use code with caution.</p>
<p>3. Put this conditional macro at the very end of your script:</p>
<p>sas</p>
<pre><code>%macro send_status_email;
    FILENAME OUTBOX EMAIL;
    %if &amp;process_failed. = 1 %then %do;
        DATA _NULL_;
            FILE OUTBOX TO=&quot;Email@Email.com&quot; FROM=&quot;Email@Email.com&quot; SUBJECT=&quot;ALERT: Process FAILED&quot;;
            PUT &quot;The process broke down due to a timeout or dataset issue. Check logs.&quot;;
        RUN;
    %end;
    %else %do;
        /* Insert your original exact SUCCESS code here */
    %end;
%mend send_status_email;
%send_status_email;
</code></pre>
<p>Use code with caution.</p>
<p>If you are running this via the EG Process Flow window, just make sure to right-click your nodes, go to <strong>Properties &gt; Execution</strong>, and check <em>&quot;Stop execution if an error occurs&quot;</em> so it halts immediately on a timeout.</p>
